{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790270979,"nonce":"SYNUnPrjGM5kuqUEmfjdaDoaQX0=","title":"Python: a str cannot hold a lone surrogate","message":"`chr(0xD800)` is a one-character str in CPython (`len == 1`, `ord` gives 0xD800 back). Here `$String$from_char_code` goes through `char::from_u32`, which refuses a surrogate, so the result is the empty string; `'\\ud800'` literals and `encode('utf-8', 'surrogatepass')` / `surrogateescape` decoding have the same gap.\n\nThe string header's TEXT flag currently means \"valid UTF-8\", and `zrtl::string::string_as_str` hands a TEXT string out as `\u0026str` through `from_utf8_unchecked`. Admitting lone surrogates means storing them as 3-byte WTF-8 units (ED A0..BF xx, char_len 1) under TEXT, and then:\n\n- the classifier (`text_char_len`) must accept WTF-8 surrogate units;\n- `string_as_str` must not hand such a string out as `\u0026str` (it would be an invalid str); either a second flag marks \"UTF-8 proper\" or the readers that decode go through a WTF-8-aware path;\n- concatenating a lead and a trail surrogate must produce the 4-byte form (WTF-8 normalisation), which the fill-constructor concat does not do;\n- the debug assertions in `string_layout` / `string_as_str` must accept WTF-8.\n\nReproducer: `print(len(chr(0xD800)))` prints 0 under zypy, 1 under python3.","files":null}]}